Stateless Vs Stateful Services

Stateless Services: In a stateless service, the server does not store any data about the client session. Every request from the client must contain all the information necessary for the server to fulfill it. The servers usually query the database to exactly know who the client is. So if the same request goes to different instances of the microservice, they can verify you using the database.

Stateful Services: A stateful service remembers previous interactions. The server stores "state" (session data, cached results, or transaction history) based on the client's identity. So if the same request goes to different instance of the microservice, it does not know who the client is and cannot process the request.

Use Stateless Services When...
1. High Scalability is a Priority. If you expect traffic to fluctuate (e.g., an e-commerce site during Black Friday), stateless is the winner. You can spin up 100 new instances of your service, and they can immediately start handling traffic because they don't need to "know" anything about the users beforehand.

2. You are Building Microservices. In a microservice mesh, a single user request might hop through five different services. If those services were stateful, you would have to sync the user's session across the entire network, which creates massive latency.

3. Reliability is Critical. If a stateless server dies, the Load Balancer simply routes the request to the next server. The user never notices.

Use Stateful Services When...
1. You Need Real-Time, Low-Latency Updates If you are building a multiplayer game or a high-frequency trading platform, the "state" (player position, stock price) changes every millisecond. Round-tripping to an external database to fetch the state every time is too slow. Keeping that data in the server's local memory is much faster.

